WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:05.170
Penguins and crushingly high G forces don't
mix – or, at least, they shouldn't.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.909
But in The Batman," the Penguin escapes a
brush with death a little too easily.

00:00:08.909 --> 00:00:10.690
Let's talk about that scene.

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:12.290
Spoilers for "The Batman" ahead.

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:16.500
When "The Batman" kicks off, Bruce Wayne has
been patrolling Gotham for two years.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.190
He's already cemented himself as a brutal
force on the streets, and he's created a sense

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:24.080
of paranoia in the city's criminal element
-- so whenever they see his symbol in the

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.060
sky, they know he could strike from the shadows
at any moment.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.849
He's a savage fighter, as seen in the film's
opening sequence with the skull-faced thugs

00:00:30.849 --> 00:00:32.879
who target a passenger on the subway.

00:00:32.879 --> 00:00:36.550
But Bruce isn't just up against petty criminals
in the 2022 film.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:41.480
He's also investigating a terrifying serial
killer called the Riddler who goes after corruption

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.280
at the highest levels of civil authority.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.070
Yes, that's a strangely heroic cause for a
murderer who terrorizes his victims with bizarre

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:52.580
traps and explosive collars, but Gotham has
plenty of room for more than one costumed

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:53.750
vigilante.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:57.239
Since the city is full to the brim with other
criminals, it's not so surprising that the

00:00:57.239 --> 00:01:00.770
Dark Knight meets some other iconic characters
over the course of the movie.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:05.370
Chief among them is Selina Kyle, aka Catwoman,
who is really more of an anti-hero than a

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:06.730
proper villain.

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:10.910
Another villain is notorious crime boss Carmine
Falcone, who sits at the heart of Gotham's

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:12.020
underworld.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:17.580
Last but certainly not least is the charismatic
Oswald Cobblepot, aka Penguin -- a gangster

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.400
who is pulled into Batman's investigation
into the Riddler.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.410
The Penguin has quite a few meaty scenes in
Matt Reeves' first Batman flick, but one of

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:26.900
those scenes doesn't really make any sense.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:30.920
One of the biggest action sequences of the
film sees Batman chase the Penguin in a high-speed

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:34.990
pursuit down the Gotham freeway after he suspects
the mobster of being an informant for the

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:35.990
GCPD.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:40.001
The dramatic scene causes plenty of chaos
on the road, and gives fans a look at what

00:01:40.001 --> 00:01:42.400
the new Batmobile is capable of.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.290
It's one of the best action sequences in the
entire film, with the cinematography dropping

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:49.190
the audience right in the middle of the swerving
vehicles.

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:53.690
Batman even uses a ruined truck to ramp through
an explosion, like a real comic book hero.

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:57.570
And when he plows into Penguin's car, the
crash that results is brutal, with the mobster's

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:01.240
vehicle spinning violently through the air
before he lands upside down.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:06.100
Cobblepot is trapped in his car, unable to
escape as Batman walks toward the wreckage.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:10.959
Luckily, Penguin only has a few minor injuries,
so Batman and James Gordon are able to interrogate

00:02:10.959 --> 00:02:12.450
him afterward without too much hassle.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:15.120
So why is this scene so troubling?

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.129
Honestly, because it's more than a little
shocking that Penguin isn't killed, or at

00:02:19.129 --> 00:02:23.209
least leaving that car in a stretcher, considering
how disastrous the crash is.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:27.849
The absurdity of it kinda challenges our suspension
of disbelief -- even in a film where a grown

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:32.230
man dressed like a bat enacts vigilante justice
against a riddling serial killer.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:35.909
So why doesn't Penguin die in "The Batman"?

00:02:35.909 --> 00:02:37.090
"Justice.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:38.579
The answer's justice."

00:02:38.579 --> 00:02:41.340
It's not justice, but there are answers.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.890
The first reason is that, from an effects
perspective, it's just a fun, cinematic way

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:45.920
to show a car crash.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.090
You could ask the same question about any
modern action scene.

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:53.379
Why doesn't Bruce Wayne get whiplash when
he totals his Lamborghini in The Dark Knight?

00:02:53.379 --> 00:02:56.260
How does anyone survive the Fast and Furious
movies?

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:57.719
It's about the spectacle.

00:02:57.719 --> 00:03:01.810
The other answer is even more obvious: Colin
Farrell has more to do with the role.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:05.290
Remember, this is only the second year of
Batman's crime fighting career -- so there's

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:09.230
still plenty of time for Cobblepot to become
the version of the Penguin that DC fans know

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:10.230
and love.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:15.099
The 2022 movie depicts him as an up-and-coming
crime boss underneath Carmine Falcone, and

00:03:15.099 --> 00:03:19.249
he's also in charge of the sleazy Iceberg
Lounge where Gotham's corrupt elite hang out

00:03:19.249 --> 00:03:20.579
most nights.

00:03:20.579 --> 00:03:24.239
The film ends with a bit of a power vacuum
in the world of organized crime.

00:03:24.239 --> 00:03:27.639
It's highly likely that Penguin will climb
the ranks to become the new crime boss if

00:03:27.639 --> 00:03:30.909
Warner Bros. gives director Matt Reeves the
green light for a sequel.

00:03:30.909 --> 00:03:35.250
The film's early reception has been overwhelmingly
positive, so hopefully the cast and crew get

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:36.930
to come back for round two.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:40.709
But there's another key reason that Penguin
isn't killed off in the film: He's getting

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:43.080
his own TV series on HBO Max.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:47.459
Per Variety, the Penguin show will be executively
produced by Matt Reeves, Dylan Clark, and

00:03:47.459 --> 00:03:48.709
Colin Farrell.

00:03:48.709 --> 00:03:52.030
Hopefully fans won't have to wait too long
until the streaming service reveals more about

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:56.239
what to expect from Farrell's spin-off series,
but until then, we can all be glad that this

00:03:56.239 --> 00:03:57.939
particular penguin is car-crash proof.

00:03:57.939 --> 00:03:58.939
"Hahaha, I got you.

00:03:58.939 --> 00:03:59.939
Hahaha.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:00.939
I got you!"

